V3DRender 0.04: kit 3, the rest of the GPU's job types
======================================================

Kit 2 showed that V3DRender drives the Pi 4's GPU: frames cleared through
its queues, MMU and interrupt, and a reset after a hang. Kit 3 tries
everything else Mesa will use, before Mesa does:

- the TFU (texture formatting unit), which Mesa uses for mipmaps and
  copies: a raster image converted into each of the GPU's tiled layouts
  (lineartile, UIF, UIF with XOR), every pixel checked against where
  Mesa's own code says it belongs;
- a compute job (what GLES 3.1 compute shaders become): a tiny program
  for the GPU's processors, assembled with Mesa's own encoder, that
  stores a value in memory;
- the binner running out of memory on a 1080p frame, so that V3DRender's
  interrupt has to give it more;
- the GPU reached through RISC3D, as Mesa will reach it (RISC3D 0.03 is
  in this folder: it is loaded if yours is older or not loaded).

Before you start
- A Pi 4, Pi 400 or CM4 with RISC OS 5.30 or later. V3DDriver not
  loaded. Copy this folder to a hard disc.

The test
1. Double-click Check. It loads V3DRender 0.04 (replacing 0.02 or 0.03
   if loaded) and RISC3D 0.03, then runs V3DCheck and V3DJob. Tests 1-2
   are kit 2's; 4-7 are new. Both programs write to Results, and
   *V3DRender's figures are added at the end.
2. Optional: double-click HangTest again (as in kit 2).
3. Send Results back.

The compute job is the first program to run on the GPU's own processors
under V3DRender. If one of the new tests freezes the machine, restart
and say which number it had reached.
